Deliberation round 4 confirms a universal consensus that Statements B, C, and D are not necessarily true, eliminating choices B through H. The remaining disagreement between Choice A and Choice I centers entirely on whether Statement A constitutes an inviolable upper bound. While classical quantitative genetics under strict orthogonality bounds variance explained by broad-sense heritability, life-history characteristics of K-selected species combined with typical population-based GWAS designs allow polygenic scores to capture indirect genetic effects and passive gene-environment covariance, enabling predictive variance to exceed direct heritability. Consequently, Statement A is not necessarily true, and Choice I (None of the other answer choices are correct) remains the most theoretically consistent and robust answer.
}

