The deliberation remains centered on Choice I versus Choice A, with complete consensus that Statements B, C, and D are not necessarily true. The debate turns entirely on Statement A: whether the combination of a K-selected species and typical GWAS data introduces indirect genetic effects (genetic nurture) that allow a polygenic score's explained variance to exceed broad-sense heritability, or whether the theoretical ideal under HWE strictly enforces an orthogonal ceiling of 50%. Given that life-history characteristics of K-selected species and standard population-based GWAS methodologies permit gene-environment covariance, Statement A is not necessarily true across all valid biological interpretations. Consequently, Choice I ("None of the other answer choices are correct") remains the most robust and defensible position.
}

